In ordinary Moth code, comments begin with two minus signs:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- This source-code line is ignored by the compiler.
    score = 10
]

Template and moth template bodies are content. Two minus signs inside those bodies
are text rather than source comments.

Use `regular_snake_case` for values and functions:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    player_score = 10

    double_score |score I32| -> I32:
        return score * 2
    ;
]

Use `PascalCase` for named types:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    PlayerScore = |
        value I32,
    |
]

Clear names beat short names. `player_score` is easier to understand than
`ps`, especially when the code grows.

`Config` and spellings differing only by case or leading underscores
are reserved by the shared naming rules. `export` is an ordinary identifier
even though `$export` is a compiler directive.
